 "segments": [
  {
   "start": 0.0,
   "end": 0.38,
   "text": "Britain."
  },
  {
   "start": 0.66,
   "end": 2.1,
   "text": "The edge of the Roman world."
  },
  {
   "start": 2.36,
   "end": 6.04,
   "text": "A young surveyor named Marcus squints through a strange wooden cross."
  },
  {
   "start": 6.32,
   "end": 8.98,
   "text": "Behind him, a column of soldiers is waiting."
  },
  {
   "start": 9.28,
   "end": 11.06,
   "text": "In front of him, a swamp."
  },
  {
   "start": 11.46,
   "end": 12.18,
   "text": "Straight through,\""
  },
  {
   "start": 12.44,
   "end": 13.26,
   "text": "says Marcus."
  },
  {
   "start": 13.56,
   "end": 15.14,
   "text": "The centurion turns purple."
  },
  {
   "start": 15.54,
   "end": 16.4,
   "text": "Through the SWAMP?\""
  },
  {
   "start": 16.84,
   "end": 19.42,
   "text": "A local guide named Bran leans on his staff."
  },
  {
   "start": 19.7,
   "end": 22.32,
   "text": "His goat is already taking the long way around."
  },
  {
   "start": 22.6,
   "end": 23.8,
   "text": "Marcus doesn't blink."
  },
  {
   "start": 24.12,
   "end": 25.34,
   "text": "The line says straight,"
  },
  {
   "start": 25.54,
   "end": 26.86,
   "text": "so the road goes straight."
  },
  {
   "start": 27.18,
   "end": 28.72,
   "text": "But something is wrong here."
  },
  {
   "start": 28.98,
   "end": 31.84,
   "text": "Why would anyone march a road straight into a swamp?"
  },
  {
   "start": 32.16,
   "end": 33.98,
   "text": "To answer that, let's go back."
  },
  {
   "start": 34.24,
   "end": 34.8,
   "text": "Way back."
  },
  {
   "start": 35.08,
   "end": 36.16,
   "text": "Here's the question."
  },
  {
   "start": 36.46,
   "end": 38.24,
   "text": "Why were Roman roads so straight?"
  },
  {
   "start": 38.5,
   "end": 40.64,
   "text": "Look at a map of the empire at its peak."
  },
  {
   "start": 40.9,
   "end": 42.72,
   "text": "Roads run from Britain to Syria,"
  },
  {
   "start": 42.9,
   "end": 44.38,
   "text": "from Spain to the Black Sea."
  },
  {
   "start": 44.66,
   "end": 47.94,
   "text": "About eighty thousand kilometers of them were paved with stone."
  },
  {
   "start": 48.26,
   "end": 50.56,
   "text": "That's enough to wrap around the Earth twice."
  },
  {
   "start": 50.82,
   "end": 54.2,
   "text": "And on the map, many of them look like they were drawn with a ruler."
  },
  {
   "start": 54.48,
   "end": 56.1,
   "text": "Most ancient paths wander."
  },
  {
   "start": 56.4,
   "end": 60.62,
   "text": "They follow rivers, dodge hills, and go around the farmer who won't sell."
  },
  {
   "start": 60.9,
   "end": 61.92,
   "text": "Roman roads just…"
  },
  {
   "start": 62.16,
   "end": 62.52,
   "text": "don't."
  },
  {
   "start": 62.78,
   "end": 70.22,
   "text": "It starts in 312 BC. Rome is a regional power in central Italy, stuck in a long, ugly war with the Samnites,"
  },
  {
   "start": 70.42,
   "end": 72.12,
   "text": "tough mountain people to the south."
  },
  {
   "start": 72.42,
   "end": 73.94,
   "text": "Rome's problem is distance."
  },
  {
   "start": 74.22,
   "end": 76.66,
   "text": "Its troops need to reach the fighting fast."
  },
  {
   "start": 76.94,
   "end": 79.78,
   "text": "So a censor named Appius Claudius orders a road."
  },
  {
   "start": 80.04,
   "end": 80.76,
   "text": "Not a trail."
  },
  {
   "start": 81.04,
   "end": 81.52,
   "text": "A road."
  },
  {
   "start": 81.8,
   "end": 84.88,
   "text": "A real engineered highway, heading south toward Capua."
  },
  {
   "start": 85.2,
   "end": 86.64,
   "text": "It's called the Via Appia."
  },
  {
   "start": 86.92,
   "end": 88.88,
   "text": "He even names it after himself."
  },
  {
   "start": 89.16,
   "end": 90.7,
   "text": "And it does something bold."
  },
  {
   "start": 90.98,
   "end": 96.74,
   "text": "From Rome to the coastal town of Terracina, it runs almost dead straight for about fifty-six miles."
  },
  {
   "start": 97.02,
   "end": 97.42,
   "text": "Hills?"
  },
  {
   "start": 97.76,
   "end": 98.34,
   "text": "Cut through."
  },
  {
   "start": 98.6,
   "end": 99.14,
   "text": "Marshes?"
  },
  {
   "start": 99.42,
   "end": 100.2,
   "text": "Built across."
  },
  {
   "start": 100.46,
   "end": 102.76,
   "text": "Rome just discovered its favorite weapon."
  },
  {
   "start": 103.04,
   "end": 104.82,
   "text": "So the obvious answer is speed."
  },
  {
   "start": 105.1,
   "end": 108.16,
   "text": "A straight line is the shortest path between two points."
  },
  {
   "start": 108.44,
   "end": 111.12,
   "text": "And a Roman legion on a good road could move fast."
  },
  {
   "start": 111.38,
   "end": 116.98,
   "text": "One Roman military manual says recruits should march about twenty Roman miles in five summer hours."
  },
  {
   "start": 117.26,
   "end": 121.92,
   "text": "Meanwhile, Bran's people use old trackways that curve around every hill and bog."
  },
  {
   "start": 122.2,
   "end": 123.88,
   "text": "Shorter road, faster army,"
  },
  {
   "start": 124.06,
   "end": 125.1,
   "text": "quicker conquest."
  },
  {
   "start": 125.4,
   "end": 126.24,
   "text": "Case closed?"
  },
  {
   "start": 126.5,
   "end": 127.22,
   "text": "Not quite."
  },
  {
   "start": 127.5,
   "end": 128.54,
   "text": "Here's the problem."
  },
  {
   "start": 128.82,
   "end": 130.44,
   "text": "Building straight is expensive."
  },
  {
   "start": 130.74,
   "end": 132.28,
   "text": "Going around a hill is cheap."
  },
  {
   "start": 132.6,
   "end": 134.64,
   "text": "Cutting through it takes months of digging."
  },
  {
   "start": 134.9,
   "end": 138.5,
   "text": "And on the steepest hills, Roman roads didn't go straight at all."
  },
  {
   "start": 138.78,
   "end": 140.34,
   "text": "So if speed was everything…"
  },
  {
   "start": 140.58,
   "end": 142.24,
   "text": "why weren't they straight everywhere?"
  },
  {
   "start": 142.56,
   "end": 143.34,
   "text": "Back in Britain,"
  },
  {
   "start": 143.54,
   "end": 145.16,
   "text": "the centurion is still yelling."
  },
  {
   "start": 145.56,
   "end": 147.34,
   "text": "My men are not swimming to work!\""
  },
  {
   "start": 147.76,
   "end": 149.96,
   "text": "Bran points to a hilltop far away."
  },
  {
   "start": 150.22,
   "end": 152.36,
   "text": "A thin line of smoke rises from it."
  },
  {
   "start": 152.64,
   "end": 154.4,
   "text": "Then another, on the next hill."
  },
  {
   "start": 154.72,
   "end": 156.42,
   "text": "And another. Bran frowns."
  },
  {
   "start": 156.72,
   "end": 158.54,
   "text": "He's seen Romans do this before."
  },
  {
   "start": 158.82,
   "end": 159.68,
   "text": "Lighting fires."
  },
  {
   "start": 160.0,
   "end": 160.9,
   "text": "Planting poles."
  },
  {
   "start": 161.2,
   "end": 163.4,
   "text": "Staring through that wooden cross for hours."
  },
  {
   "start": 163.68,
   "end": 165.78,
   "text": "He thinks it's some kind of religion."
  },
  {
   "start": 166.08,
   "end": 166.98,
   "text": "He's half right."
  },
  {
   "start": 167.28,
   "end": 170.8,
   "text": "Because the real reason Roman roads are straight isn't just the army."
  },
  {
   "start": 171.08,
   "end": 171.92,
   "text": "It's that stick."
  },
  {
   "start": 172.2,
   "end": 172.92,
   "text": "Meet the groma."
  },
  {
   "start": 173.2,
   "end": 177.7,
   "text": "It's a pole with a wooden cross on top, and four weights hanging from strings."
  },
  {
   "start": 178.04,
   "end": 178.52,
   "text": "That's it."
  },
  {
   "start": 178.8,
   "end": 179.58,
   "text": "No lenses."
  },
  {
   "start": 179.84,
   "end": 180.62,
   "text": "No compass."
  },
  {
   "start": 180.88,
   "end": 181.64,
   "text": "No GPS."
  },
  {
   "start": 181.94,
   "end": 184.62,
   "text": "The strings hang straight down because of gravity."
  },
  {
   "start": 184.94,
   "end": 190.46,
   "text": "So when Marcus lines up two strings with his eye, he gets a perfectly straight line of sight."
  },
  {
   "start": 190.74,
   "end": 193.14,
   "text": "An assistant walks ahead with a tall pole."
  },
  {
   "start": 193.52,
   "end": 193.9,
   "text": "Left."
  },
  {
   "start": 194.16,
   "end": 194.54,
   "text": "Left."
  },
  {
   "start": 194.84,
   "end": 195.24,
   "text": "Stop!\""
  },
  {
   "start": 195.72,
   "end": 197.58,
   "text": "Plant it. Then another pole,"
  },
  {
   "start": 197.78,
   "end": 198.46,
   "text": "further on."
  },
  {
   "start": 198.74,
   "end": 201.52,
   "text": "When all the poles line up, they hide each other."
  },
  {
   "start": 201.8,
   "end": 203.14,
   "text": "You see just one pole."
  },
  {
   "start": 203.46,
   "end": 205.5,
   "text": "That's how you know the line is straight."
  },
  {
   "start": 205.78,
   "end": 206.7,
   "text": "Here's the catch."
  },
  {
   "start": 206.98,
   "end": 209.52,
   "text": "This tool is brilliant at exactly one thing."
  },
  {
   "start": 209.8,
   "end": 210.68,
   "text": "Straight lines."
  },
  {
   "start": 210.96,
   "end": 211.98,
   "text": "Now scale it up."
  },
  {
   "start": 212.3,
   "end": 216.74,
   "text": "To link two forts many miles apart, surveyors couldn't see one from the other."
  },
  {
   "start": 217.02,
   "end": 220.2,
   "text": "So, historians think, they worked from high ground."
  },
  {
   "start": 220.48,
   "end": 222.56,
   "text": "A fire or a pole on one hilltop."
  },
  {
   "start": 222.84,
   "end": 226.06,
   "text": "Another on the next. Smoke by day, flames by night."
  },
  {
   "start": 226.38,
   "end": 227.86,
   "text": "Then they filled in the gaps,"
  },
  {
   "start": 228.06,
   "end": 231.28,
   "text": "pole by pole. The result is not one perfect line."
  },
  {
   "start": 231.54,
   "end": 235.78,
   "text": "It's a chain of straight segments, and each one bends a little at a hilltop."
  },
  {
   "start": 236.08,
   "end": 237.74,
   "text": "Look at the Fosse Way in Britain."
  },
  {
   "start": 238.02,
   "end": 241.64,
   "text": "It runs about two hundred miles, from Lincoln down to Devon."
  },
  {
   "start": 241.9,
   "end": 246.42,
   "text": "And yet it never strays more than about six miles from a single straight line."
  },
  {
   "start": 246.7,
   "end": 249.28,
   "text": "Not bad for sticks, string, and smoke. Bran's goat,"
  },
  {
   "start": 249.56,
   "end": 253.34,
   "text": "meanwhile, has walked three miles and gotten nowhere."
  },
  {
   "start": 253.64,
   "end": 255.08,
   "text": "So here's the real answer."
  },
  {
   "start": 255.34,
   "end": 259.2,
   "text": "Roman roads were straight because straight was the easiest thing to survey."
  },
  {
   "start": 259.52,
   "end": 260.84,
   "text": "With a groma and poles,"
  },
  {
   "start": 261.02,
   "end": 264.48,
   "text": "a straight line is simple and almost impossible to get wrong."
  },
  {
   "start": 264.78,
   "end": 265.64,
   "text": "A smooth curve?"
  },
  {
   "start": 265.92,
   "end": 269.86,
   "text": "That takes far more measuring, and good maps they mostly didn't have."
  },
  {
   "start": 270.16,
   "end": 271.04,
   "text": "Then add power."
  },
  {
   "start": 271.32,
   "end": 278.7,
   "text": "Rome had soldiers to do the digging, and it rarely asked conquered locals for permission. Bogs got filled with timber and stone."
  },
  {
   "start": 278.98,
   "end": 282.92,
   "text": "Back at the swamp, the soldiers start sinking logs into the mud."
  },
  {
   "start": 283.2,
   "end": 284.2,
   "text": "Rivers got bridges."
  },
  {
   "start": 284.5,
   "end": 291.36,
   "text": "And when a hill was truly too steep for carts and mules, the surveyors gave up on straight and zigzagged up the slope."
  },
  {
   "start": 291.64,
   "end": 292.96,
   "text": "Straight wasn't a religion."
  },
  {
   "start": 293.22,
   "end": 295.12,
   "text": "It was the default setting of the tool."
  },
  {
   "start": 295.42,
   "end": 296.84,
   "text": "So Bran was half right."
  },
  {
   "start": 297.1,
   "end": 299.0,
   "text": "And those lines outlived the empire."
  },
  {
   "start": 299.28,
   "end": 302.78,
   "text": "In Britain, modern roads still run on top of Roman routes,"
  },
  {
   "start": 302.96,
   "end": 306.48,
   "text": "like Watling Street, now under long stretches of the A5."
  },
  {
   "start": 306.78,
   "end": 310.72,
   "text": "Drive them today and you'll feel it: the road just refuses to turn."
  },
  {
   "start": 311.02,
   "end": 314.18,
   "text": "Meanwhile, back at the swamp, the new road is finished."
  },
  {
   "start": 314.48,
   "end": 315.52,
   "text": "Marcus walks it,"
  },
  {
   "start": 315.7,
   "end": 316.1,
   "text": "proud."
  },
  {
   "start": 316.38,
   "end": 317.9,
   "text": "The centurion marches by,"
  },
  {
   "start": 318.08,
   "end": 318.96,
   "text": "dry for once."
  },
  {
   "start": 319.22,
   "end": 320.12,
   "text": "And Bran's goat?"
  },
  {
   "start": 320.42,
   "end": 322.34,
   "text": "It stops at the edge of the stone."
  },
  {
   "start": 322.64,
   "end": 324.82,
   "text": "Looks left at its winding old path."
  },
  {
   "start": 325.1,
   "end": 326.42,
   "text": "Looks right at the road."
  },
  {
   "start": 326.7,
   "end": 328.46,
   "text": "And trots straight down the middle."
  },
  {
   "start": 328.8,
   "end": 330.8,
   "text": "So why were Roman roads so straight?"
  },
  {
   "start": 331.08,
   "end": 334.08,
   "text": "Because a stick and some string could only see straight."
  },
  {
   "start": 334.36,
   "end": 337.62,
   "text": "And Rome was stubborn enough to build whatever the stick saw."
  }
 ],
 "duration": 337.91,
